<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌤️ LIVE VISIBILITY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/visibility/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t21z-vis-anl-g8" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Meteorological visibility in statute miles (HRRR surface analysis, FAA flight-category scale): <b>0-0.25 Dense fog</b> maroon (NWS Dense Fog Advisory, 1/4 mi or less) &rarr; <b>0.25-1 LIFR</b> red &rarr; <b>1-3 IFR</b> orange &rarr; <b>3-5 MVFR</b> gold &rarr; <b>5-30 VFR</b> green to deep blue (10SM is the METAR reporting cap; the model resolves past it). Same visibility always shows the same color; above 30 mi clamps into deep blue. Human-observer/mariner/pilot visibility through haze, mist, precipitation, and smoke — not astronomical seeing.</p><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km surface visibility analysis, hourly cycles<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE VISIBILITY</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/visibility/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t21z-vis-anl-g8" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Meteorological visibility in statute miles (HRRR surface analysis, FAA flight-category scale): <b>0-0.25 Dense fog</b> maroon (NWS Dense Fog Advisory, 1/4 mi or less) &rarr; <b>0.25-1 LIFR</b> red &rarr; <b>1-3 IFR</b> orange &rarr; <b>3-5 MVFR</b> gold &rarr; <b>5-30 VFR</b> green to deep blue (10SM is the METAR reporting cap; the model resolves past it). Same visibility always shows the same color; above 30 mi clamps into deep blue. Human-observer/mariner/pilot visibility through haze, mist, precipitation, and smoke — not astronomical seeing.</p><p>Near-surface meteorological visibility from the operational HRRR analysis: how far a human observer, mariner, or pilot can distinguish surroundings through haze, mist, precipitation, and smoke. Low values flag navigation and shoreline hazards; high values mark crystal-clear Great Lakes air.</p><p>Visibility science (atmospheric optics and observing practice) quantifies horizontal contrast loss from scattering and absorption by hydrometeors and aerosols. It underpins aviation flight rules, marine navigation advisories, and air-quality perception, and is reported operationally in statute miles across the United States.</p><p><b>Units:</b> mi, statute (display; source m / 1609.344)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km surface visibility analysis, hourly cycles<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
      <GroundOverlay>
        <name>🌤️ LIVE VISIBILITY</name>
        <drawOrder>10</drawOrder>
        <Icon>
          <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/visibility/current.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t21z-vis-anl-g8</href>
          <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
          <refreshInterval>3600</refreshInterval>
        </Icon>
        <LatLonBox>
          <north>49.5</north>
          <south>40.5</south>
          <east>-73.5</east>
          <west>-93.0</west>
        </LatLonBox>
      </GroundOverlay>
    </Folder>
